epic         V3
title        Flow State v3 production
status       active
description
    **Goal:** "production code for Flow State v3 at high quality, with good error messages and the correctness
    guarantees the prototypes proved, plus example apps and generated documentation" (the user, 2026-11-04). Built with
    skill:to-epic at the library revision in skill, and rewritten from scratch on 2026-11-15 after the conformance suite
    was deleted.

    Records: Plans @plan.FlowState, @plan.Machines, @plan.Operations, @plan.Runtime, @plan.Persistence,
    @plan.EntityDomains, @plan.React, @plan.ServerRendering, @plan.Testing, @plan.Inspection, @plan.Diagnostics,
    @plan.CommandLine, @plan.LazyFeatures, @plan.Package (status proposed), @plan.Rules. v3 Specs @spec.Schedules,
    @spec.Changes, @spec.Records, @spec.Operations, @spec.Types, @spec.Host (each with a companion `.md` in
    `wiki/specs/v3/`); the core Specs in `wiki/specs/core/` are superseded. Deferred and open items:
    @revisit.revisit, @revisit.open-items.

    Locations:

    - production code `packages/flow-state-v3/src/`: `src/core/` and the files beside it are the pure core, `src/host/`
      is the Effect host, and `src/host/testing/` is the test tooling;
    - fixtures `packages/flow-state-v3/fixtures/`, shared by the tests and the bench;
    - Flow's own tests `packages/flow-state-v3/tests/`; the bench `packages/flow-state-v3/bench/`;
    - the prototype reference `packages/flow-state-v3/prototypes/round-14/`;
    - proposal files `wiki/plans/flow/references/proposals/`; the test system's design is @proposal.test-system, and the
      tools' API is @proposal.test-tooling-reuse;
    - reference apps `wiki/plans/flow/references/checkout/` and `dispatch-desk/`, guides where the P2 card says;
    - example apps `examples/` (P6); docs `apps/docs/` (P7).

    ## Stages and stage exits

    | Stage | Phases | Stage exit |
    | --- | --- | --- |
    | A Settled | P1 | Done: design and prototype agree; Plans and core Specs written; Pay path runs on production |
    | B Test system | P2 | Flow's tests run on its own tooling; one test style is left; every named fault is caught |
    | C Core | P3 | Every built core Requirement holds on production, each shown by a check failing on a planted fault |
    | D Preview | P4 | Fixtures (Pay, checkout) run in Chrome, Safari; `flow check` works; `nub run verify` builds v3 |
    | E Features | P5 | Each feature's Spec is written, then holds on production |
    | F Examples and docs | P6, P7 | Example apps run in real Chrome and Safari; docs generated from source |
    | G Hardening and release | P8, P9, P10 | Budgets met, findings swept, released as far as the user asks |

    ## Scope

    Bounds: P1 to P10; UI is React and Next.js only. Assumptions: the prototype is the reference implementation for what
    production has not built. Exclusions: Electron; TanStack DB integration; tla-ts; new prototyping; feature Specs
    before P5, except the test tooling's, which P2 writes; migration functions and manual versions (revisit.md). Reused
    evidence: stage A's results (P1), and the 81 hand cases that pass on production, kept in `tests/bridge/` until P2
    ports them.

    Order of authority: Specs, then Plans; where a Plan and the prototype disagree, the prototype wins until a Spec
    records it. Specs and Plans are guides: when building shows one wrong, it is fixed with a logged reason, not obeyed.

    ## Constraints

    - One runtime, one way to run it: Flow's own tests run on the test tooling users will get (Story, Fake, the runtime
      cut and trace, the CLI), so both are hardened. No model of the core, second runtime, gate file, list of owed
      checks or recorded baseline of test output is built (the user, 2026-11-15; whole run). Performance baselines (P8,
      P9) are not test output.
    - Keep it stupid simple: plain functions and plain tests; no tool, runner or ledger where a test or a grep does the
      job (the user, 2026-11-15; whole run).
    - Set up before building: work goes in Tasks, and the layout and import rules are in place before code is written
      against them (the user, 2026-11-15; whole run).
    - No new prototyping; production may converge again (session, 2026-11-04; whole run).
    - The core and its tests are written cleanly, with the prototype open beside them; prototype code never moves into
      the package (session, 2026-11-04; P2 to P5).
    - Production code is built from `.spec` files written with skill:to-design and skill:to-spec; briefs cite the Spec,
      reviews check code against it (the user, 2026-11-09; whole run).
    - Specs are written just in time and revised whenever building shows them wrong, with the reason logged (the user,
      2026-11-09; whole run).
    - Production features are built test-first (memory, 2026-11-04; P2 to P10).
    - Test the spec, not the code: expected results come from a cited Spec rule, are written before the code, and never
      come from running the code; recorded output is a change detector only (memory, 2026-11-04; whole run).
    - Every public feature in the Plans is represented in a fixture, and the same fixtures feed the tests and the bench
      (the user, 2026-11-11; whole run).
    - The package is self-contained: its code reads and imports nothing outside it, and names no temporary record (a
      dated proposal, a review, a Task or step id), in code or in comments (the user, 2026-11-14; whole run).
    - Code style: every brief carries AGENTS.md "Code style (v3 production)", lint-bomb's `tools/lint-bomb/STYLE.md` and
      `tools/lint-bomb/FLOW-STATE.md`; the core is plain, pure, synchronous and deterministic, returning work as data;
      Effect only in `src/host/` and the edges; doc comments only on exported API; `nub run check:fix`, then `nub run
      check` at zero errors and warnings (AGENTS.md; the user, 2026-11-09; P2 to P10).
    - Write idiomatic Effect and build nothing parallel to it; an Effect API is checked against the installed source
      with the project's `effect-api-documentation` skill, and new host code gets an idiomatic-Effect review (the user,
      2026-11-11 and 2026-11-15; whole run).
    - No plan or invariant ids in production code; the Spec owns them and tests cite them (session, 2026-11-04;
      whole run).
    - Lint covers the whole package: `src`, `tests`, `fixtures` and `bench` (the user, 2026-11-09; whole run).
    - UI is React and Next.js only; Electron and TanStack DB integration are out (session, 2026-11-04; whole run). The
      CLI keeps only `flow check`, `explain`, `graph`, `story` and `trace` (the user, 2026-11-02; P2 to P5).
    - Docs live in `apps/docs/`, written in the source in Effect's shape and generated to Markdown; example apps go in
      `examples/`, built with Next.js, each explaining what it demos (session, 2026-11-04; P6, P7).
    - Plans hold what is wanted, with code examples and the behaviour that matters, not prose; this is the standard when
      a Plan is next written, not a rewrite to start unasked (the user, 2026-11-15; whole run).
    - Ask in numbered rounds with before and after and a recommendation; never present a recommendation as decided; call
      him "the user" (AGENTS.md; whole run).
    - Work serially: one lead at a time. Up to 8 deterministic processes, about 16 GB; real-browser runs and benchmarks
      alone; kill only this run's processes, by exact pid (memory, 2026-11-04; whole run).
    - Business keys for money (2026-11-01; P3 to P5). Entities are optional but not up for cutting (memory, 2026-11-01;
      P5). No tla-ts (memory, 2026-11-03; whole run).

    ## Authority

    By default: read, edit within the phase's scope, run checks, repair own defects, update records. Also allowed, by
    the user's approval of this Epic:

    - commit locally to `flow-rewrite` with explicit paths; never push, never stash;
    - start agents as the loop's instructions say;
    - insert Tasks, split a phase that keeps its goal, reorder without breaking a dependency;
    - move, split or add context comments to Tasks within a slice; new slices are avoided (the user, 2026-11-22);
    - start the next phase after its completion audit passes, unless it starts with a stop;
    - write and revise Specs (skill:to-spec, skill:to-design), each change logged in `wiki/specs/specs.log` (`wk log
      add specs`).

    Orchestrator decides: design calls in P2 to P5 and P9; the internal module layout and the tests' layout; small
    public API changes (a flag, a default, a field or member name, where an option sits); small amendments to a Task's
    acceptance that keep it correct and the work going (the user, 2026-11-12). Each is noted for the user's read.

    User decides: public entry-point names; new concepts; large public API changes; relaxing a gate; changes to the
    Plans' decisions or outcomes, and any edit to a Plan (list the change and wait for the user's yes); scope; the frame
    (skill:build-a-loop#rules, R16); which example apps, and accepting each app; the CLI outputs; the docs criteria;
    budget targets; each ship stage; more agents than the instructions allow. Everything else still needs the user.

    ## Limits

    No budget of iterations, time or usage, for the Epic, a phase or a Task (the user, 2026-11-10). No failure limit
    (the user, 2026-11-22; this overrides skill:build-a-loop's default and its stop): a slice that fails again on one
    root cause after convergence does not stop the run or hold the phase. The orchestrator records the root cause and
    what is left as a comment on the slice's Tasks, leaves those Tasks open, and goes on to the next slice. Usage
    reserve: the run stops at 100 percent weekly usage, all models (the user, 2026-11-15); at the reserve the lead
    finishes its step, commits and leaves a resume note. No new slice starts with less than 5% of the weekly limit left;
    a running lead finishes (the user, 2026-11-23). Usage always means the weekly window, never the 5-hour one, read
    from the harness's usage view. The user changes the reserve at any time: a new value is an edit here and a note.

    ## Glossary

    Library terms: skill:to-epic#terms. This project's terms:

    - The conformance suite: the old private test system (a Spec model, a port, round-14 as a second runtime, owed
      lists), deleted on 2026-11-15; its last full copy is commit `52a883e6`. The Specs still name its oracles and owed
      rows in places; those lines describe it, not today's tests.
    - Story: a run scripted as an array of steps, as data. Fake: a second Layer for a service a fixture declares,
      answering from its own state.
    - Runtime cut: one read of the Runtime's state (each live actor's snapshot, lanes, timers, batches out). Checkpoint:
      a named point in a Story where the run records the time, the runtime cut, what each fake received and the store.
    - Run rule: a function from a finished run to its breaches of one Spec rule, applied to every Story run.
    - Planted fault: this project's mutant: a named wrong behaviour of the runtime with the rule it breaks, in
      `packages/flow-state-v3/tests/faults.ts`. `nub run faults` plants each in a scratch copy and reports which its own
      target tests catch; runtime rules only. Dead core: the null implementation, a `step` that changes nothing, planted
      the same way.
    - Bridge: the old hand cases rehosted in `tests/bridge/`, deleted file by file as they become Story tests.
    - Slice (the user, 2026-11-22): an execution slice, a parent Task's open children run together through one loop
      (Building a Task, Slices); a Task with no parent is its own slice. Before 2026-11-22 a slice meant one Task of P3
      or P4 built end to end, as older notes use it. The Pay path: the fixtures, including Pay and checkout, run through
      the public API.
    - Reload sweep: every fixture Story run again with a reload inserted at each position, with the run rules applied.
      Dead tab: an earlier open of a tab, abandoned by a reload; nothing from it may write or be answered afterwards.
    - Wire words: the steps that control one call to a fake (hold, release, dropRequest, dropResponse, answer). Storage
      words: the same for the fake store (hold, refuse, release).
    - Step samples: the steps a fixture offers the generator. Open calls: calls a fake has received and not
      yet answered.
    - Owed endings: a domain term of @spec.Records, an ending the Runtime still has to deliver; it is unrelated to the
      old suite's owed lists.
    - First commit of a Task: its requirements list in the Task's comments, with each requirement as a check that
      fails today.
    - The user's page: one page at a slice's close on what an app author will notice, written in
      `wiki/plans/flow/references/pages/`, one page per slice (the user, 2026-11-22).
    - Build-time choice: a block in a Task description giving two approaches, the reasoning for each and the test that
      decides; the builder runs that test, picks one and records a `[decided: agent <role> <date>]` tag with Why and
      Reverse (the user, 2026-11-22).
skill        workflow@4f316bf
run
    To start: "run the loop in wiki/tasks/V3/epic.task". You are the orchestrator: you start, track and route agents;
    you never build.

    Start and resume by skill:build-a-loop#rules R1 to R7, R25 and R26: read, current Task, cron, real agent state,
    start note, one building lead, activity, keep the machine awake (for example `caffeinate -i` on this Mac). Project
    additions to R2: a Task in `review` comes first, and its independent check starts before any builder; a phase's
    Tasks stay `draft` until it starts; before its first Task starts, run R48's breakdown review, apply its re-splits,
    then write the next Task's full text with skill:to-task and open it; within a phase, follow its card's Order line.
    Project addition to R7: a background agent's output file stays empty until it finishes, so activity is its
    transcript file growing (size and time only, never its contents), plus commits and status changes (the
    user, 2026-11-23).

    Rules: follow skill:build-a-loop#rules, skill:policy-delegation and skill:policy-verification at the library
    revision in skill; instructions override their defaults. The library is the Git repository
    `/Users/arpit/Developer/workflow/skills`, with the suite at `suites/workflow`; check its revision with `git -C
    /Users/arpit/Developer/workflow/skills rev-parse --short HEAD`, and if it differs from skill, read the pinned files
    from its Git history or ask the user.
instructions
    **Models and agents**

    - Opus leads and Haiku sidecars, Claude Code (detected 2026-11-09). Every lead runs at medium effort, except the
      requirements lead, which runs at high (the user, 2026-11-22); Haiku review sidecars at high, read-only sweeps at
      medium (the user, 2026-11-14; overrides the effort defaults of R30 and skill:policy-delegation).
    - Agents at once: one lead, building or reviewing, never both (the user, 2026-11-16; was one of each). The lead runs
      up to eight sidecars and decides how many and which jobs they do, never one per angle by default (the user,
      2026-11-14 to 2026-11-16). A review runs between builds: the builder stops at `review`, the review runs, then the
      fix round or the next Task. Extra work (merges, code-quality reviews) waits for the slot. Before starting any
      agent, the orchestrator reads the 5-hour window: above 80% only the current lead continues; above 90% it
      checkpoints and stops until the reset. Processes and browsers: the Constraints' serial-work line.
    - Settled conflicts with AGENTS.md (the user, 2026-11-22): the Epic's local commits stand and nothing is ever
      pushed; a lead may run up to eight Haiku sidecars.
    - Haiku never writes or runs production code; it may run experiments or hill-climb in scratch files outside the
      package and submit ideas (the user, 2026-11-10 and 2026-11-15). A lead reads every line a sidecar wrote (the user,
      2026-11-08); re-running and logging results is skill:policy-delegation's.
    - A lead works through three context compactions and is rotated out only when a fourth is close: finish the step,
      commit, leave a resume note (the user, 2026-11-14).
    - Who runs each step of a slice (the user, 2026-11-23): the requirements lead, at high effort, writes the
      requirements and their fix round, then stops; a fresh building lead at medium effort takes the build and every
      later fix round. A fix round's review goes back by message to the lead that wrote the verdict, so it re-runs its
      own probes; a fresh reviewer is started only when that lead is gone or past its compactions.
    - Leads report once per batch or stop, in at most 10 lines: verdict, commits, test counts, and what needs a decision
      (the user, 2026-11-11).
    - Constraints: the Epic's Constraints section. Limits: its Limits section.

    **Building a Task**

    - Every brief carries the Constraints' code-style line (the user, 2026-11-09).
    - A Task's "May change" is a guide, not a fence: a Task may add its planted faults to `tests/faults.ts` and touch
      any file its acceptance needs, naming each extra file in a Task comment (2026-11-15).
    - Slices (the user, 2026-11-22): a parent Task groups an execution slice. When a ready Task has a parent, the
      orchestrator takes all of that parent's open children together as one slice, through one requirements phase with a
      whole-slice state inventory, one requirements review, one build and one build review. The parent closes when every
      child is accepted and the slice review has checked the seams between them. Tasks stay the unit of acceptance. A
      Task with no parent is its own slice. Slices run in the order of the phase's Task order. The orchestrator may
      regroup by moving `parent`, with the reason in a V3 log note. A slice fits one builder run inside one 5-hour
      usage window.
    - Slice commands (wk): `wk show @epic.V3 --tree` lists parents with their children (a child shows `Parent:`); `wk
      show @task.V3.Tn --tree` lists one parent's children. `wk next` offers leaf Tasks only, since a parent is never
      ready: when it offers a child, take that child's parent and all its open children. `wk brief` on a parent shows
      only the phase card, so brief each child. Regroup with `wk edit @task.V3.Tn --set parent=@task.V3.Tm`; make a Task
      its own slice with `wk edit @task.V3.Tn --clear parent`. A parent may not be `blocked_by` its own children (wk
      refuses the cycle); it closes after them because it is never ready. A new slice parent is a Task titled "Slice:
      <name>" whose acceptance is every child accepted and the seams checked by the slice review.
    - Correctness (the user, 2026-11-22): every Task that builds or changes a v3 design says in its acceptance how its
      correctness is established, as one "Correctness:" item. Each of these holds: a corresponding fixture app exists
      that uses the feature (a whole app written the way a user would write it; see @convention.fixture-catalog for the
      apps and how to build them); a Story tests that fixture's feature, plus the Spec's Story of the wrong ending; a
      test runs the Story; type tests show the feature is typed fully and correctly, the way a user would expect
      (two-sided pins, and no annotations needed in user code); each new runtime rule has a planted fault its own target
      tests catch (type and lint rules have none; the user, 2026-11-22). See the existing tests
      (`packages/flow-state-v3/tests/`, `fixtures/`) for how things are done now. The item's last line names the Spec
      Requirements this Task's tests cover and the Invariants they check after every step, with the Spec tests that
      check them, by @spec id. A new Task inherits this rule and states it in the same item.
    - Requirements first (the user, 2026-11-12), by skill:execute-task step 2. The requirements list is in the Task's
      comments, each rule with its source, mapped one to one to the Task's acceptance. The lead may gather it with
      read-only sidecars on named angles (the Specs and Plans; the code and prior art; open findings; an adversary). The
      list maps every Requirement in the Task's Spec range, not only its Invariants: a rule no Invariant carries is
      otherwise missed (the user, 2026-11-23). The requirements brief names @convention.fixtures' variety rule and the
      extend-first rule below up front (the user, 2026-11-23).
    - The whole Task at once (the user, 2026-11-20): skill:execute-task step 1, with Task boundaries by skill:to-task
      step 1 and R48's phase-start review. The state's one owner function ships with its guard and its planted faults in
      the same Task; an implicit state machine spread across separate checks is a blocking finding.
    - A feature's benches are its fixture's `bench` entries, added with its Stories (the user, 2026-11-18).
    - Fixtures first (the user, 2026-11-16): a Task that builds or changes a public feature, or lands a review finding,
      meets @convention.fixtures as part of its requirements, before any code. The orchestrator owns it and checks it by
      asking the lead for that page's proof, never by reading the tests itself; a gap returns to the lead before the
      build, and the requirements review checks the proof, not the claim. Before posting the proof, the lead has
      sidecars review its fixtures against every part of that page: scenarios, load and structural stress, edge values,
      combinations, variety, real-shaped data, registration with every runner, and that each expected result is correct
      for its cited rule; the lead verifies and logs each finding (the user, 2026-11-16). Every Task with fixture work
      links the page in its acceptance, and every brief cites it too. What to build comes from
      @convention.fixture-catalog (the user, 2026-11-17): each Task's fixtures line names its migration step and
      fixtures; the lead builds those rows' scenarios from their cited sources, growing apps additively: a feature
      extends an existing machine where a real app would, and a new machine is added only when none has a natural reason
      to own it (the user, 2026-11-22). Fixture counts are set from the bench and recorded in each Story; a count may
      change only by a recorded re-measurement (the user, 2026-11-17, Q8 A). The catalog is a task list: at the close
      the lead marks every scenario item in those fixtures built (with its Story id) or left (with the later Task and
      the feature it needs), never leaves one unmarked, and corrects any row the build proved wrong; only a scenario
      needing a later Task's feature may be left, and the review checks each. The scenarios and their linked tests are
      best-guess guidance: the lead targets proper coverage and may add, reshape or replace scenarios, writing each
      change back as a catalog item.
    - Coverage before fixes and reviews (the user, 2026-11-17): a Task's bug fixing and its review wait until the
      fixtures its catalog step names are built, with every scenario written; a bug those Stories find lands as an
      inverted test with its owner Task, and is fixed by that owner against the full coverage.
    - A new runtime rule is shown red on a planted fault (the user, 2026-11-22, Q7 B): add the fault to
      `tests/faults.ts`, and `nub run faults --only <names>` must catch it with its own target tests; a fault its
      targets miss is re-aimed, never judged by the whole suite. Type and lint rules get no planted fault: their tests
      are two-sided (`@ts-expect-error`, a rule's must-flag cases). A Task plants only its own faults, by name, never
      the whole list. Plants go in a scratch copy, never the live tree.
    - Lint-driven refactors (the user, 2026-11-22): to find an old pattern to migrate or delete, plain search (`rg`) and
      search-and-replace are the first choice and suit most cases: names, imports, strings, small changes. A temporary
      lint rule is used only when the lead names why it is the right tool for this job: the pattern is structural (a
      shape, not a name) that search can't match reliably, spans many sites, or must stay gone. Such a rule goes in
      `tools/flow-state-lint/rules/` with its own test of the patterns it must and must not flag, so the hits are
      trusted. It runs on demand, outside `nub run check`, as the mechanical pass beside any agent inventory. At zero
      hits it is deleted, or promoted into `nub run check` when the pattern must never return. A sidecar may draft one
      in the scratchpad as a finder; a committed rule is written by the lead. A migration or deletion of a public form
      gets one agent inventory and one mechanical pass (`rg`, or a temporary lint rule), compared before its
      requirements are written (the user, 2026-11-23).
    - A known bug is one inverted test (`it.fails`), titled by the Spec rule it breaks; `it.todo` is not used. A gap a
      review routes to a later Task lands as an inverted test, or as a comment on its owner Task when no fixture can
      express it yet (the user, 2026-11-12).
    - Which side is wrong (the user, 2026-11-12): before a red becomes a requirement or a fix, the lead replays it and
      names the wrong side with evidence: the runtime breaks a cited rule, or the test, the fake or the tooling does. An
      unknown is never given a default.
    - Spec contradictions (the user, 2026-11-12): the lead writes one concrete case with both results and decides a
      small or internal one itself, keeping the reading that loses no ending. Running code overrides a Spec, guarantees
      included, once the change is weighed across the whole system (the user, 2026-11-15): two traces of the same Story,
      every rule and reader that depends on it checked, and the next independent review confirms it was not shaped to
      fit the code; the user reads it afterwards. The Spec is guidance and need not be kept in step with the code (the
      user, 2026-11-22): the lead records each deviation, and each call where a Spec is silent, as a `[decided: agent
      builder <date>]` tag with Why and Reverse in a Task comment, so it can be reversed; editing the Spec is optional.
      A silent Spec is never filled with the code's current behaviour as if it were the design.
    - A gap is never written into a Spec as a limit (the user, 2026-11-16): a lost, doubled or made-up ending the code
      can't yet meet lands as an inverted test titled by its rule, with an owner Task, and the Spec keeps the rule.
    - Lessons from the 2026-11-15 run (the user, 2026-11-16): comments are stamped only with `--now`, and every `.log`
      entry only with `wk log add`, never a hand-written time (the user, 2026-11-22); a gate counts by its exit code,
      never its summary line; a slice with its reviews costs about 5 to 10% of the weekly limit (slices T119 and T92
      took about 8% each; the user, 2026-11-23).
    - Plan changes found while building go to @plan.Pending as decisions, never into the Plans (the user, 2026-11-11),
      with the same whole-system check (the user, 2026-11-15).
    - Effect prior art (the user, 2026-11-11): each module's Spec skeleton lists the Effect features that cover its
      needs and why each is used or skipped. Adapters use typed failures, defects apart.
    - Speed (the user, 2026-11-14): batch independent tool calls in one message; launch a round's sidecars in one
      message; the package's suite (without the stress runs) may run whenever useful, and `nub run check` and that suite
      run before each checkpoint commit; a builder that needs a ruling states it and keeps doing independent work.
    - Big test commands run at the end of a Task (the user, 2026-11-16): the stress runs (stress Stories, their reload
      sweeps, generated stress seeds) sit behind a flag outside the default suite; they run once at a Task's close, in
      its review, and at a phase's close, never mid-Task. The whole `nub run faults` runs only on the user's demand (the
      user, 2026-11-22; was daily): no wake, Task, review or phase close starts it; a phase gate that names it asks the
      user. When it runs, it runs in the background, timings taken meanwhile are marked as such, and each missed fault
      is routed.
    - Proof has a time budget (the user, 2026-11-18): any single verification step a lead or reviewer runs (a harness, a
      sweep, a matrix) is capped at 10 minutes; past it, it stops, reports what it has, and the orchestrator decides
      whether more is worth it. A review runs the suite once, alone, plus the stress runs only when the Task touched
      stress Stories or the sweeps.
    - At a Task's close the builder runs the stress runs and the generated Stories over fresh seeds; every new failure
      lands as a fix or an inverted test before review.
    - Earlier surfacing of what close reviews kept finding (the user, 2026-11-20):
      - a design question found mid-build is validated before the build builds on it (the user, 2026-11-22): two traces
        in a Task comment and one blind Haiku sidecar on that rule (skill:execute-task step 3), and the reds it touches
        are reconciled and re-run;
      - checks walk a structure the way the cited rule defines it, never the way the code traverses it, so a test cannot
        share the code's blind spot (the user, 2026-11-23);
      - a test oracle for a boundary is derived from the writer it judges (one codec or Schema used by the writer, the
        reader and the oracle), never hand-written beside it (the user, 2026-11-23);
      - when the host mirrors a fact the core owns (a timer, a lease, a subscription), the harness checks both sides at
        every cut, not only the core's (the user, 2026-11-23);
      - an acceptance item stated as an absence ("no `it.fails` left", "no caller of X") gets an `rg` in the builder's
        close gates (the user, 2026-11-23);
      - every requirements list names each real resource the Task touches (store, page, network, clock) and what its
        fake does not yet do (close, refuse after close, hold, hide); a gap is hardened in the fake or checked for real;
      - a Task touching a persisted shape shows restore after the definition is edited: a reorder, an add, a remove and
        a rename.

    **Reviews and done**

    - Root cause first (the user, 2026-11-22): every finding a reviewer raises, and every fix or ruling a builder or the
      orchestrator makes, names where the behaviour originates, traced to its source (Flow's code, a fixture, the
      tooling, or a dependency such as Effect, checked against the installed source), before a fix or a ruling is
      proposed. A finding without its origin goes back for one. Example: `Duration.millis(NaN)` became 0 ms inside
      Effect, which settled the call at once (follow Effect) and made Flow's guards needless.
    - Reviews run on events, not on the clock (overrides R22): a Task's first commit, a Task's close, a Spec change, and
      a change to the test tooling or the run rules. One review sidecar is blind to the Task's requirements list (the
      user, 2026-11-12).
    - A requirements review has five fixed sidecars (the user, 2026-11-20), covering
      skill:check-task#at-the-requirements-review: blind A (requirements from the acceptance and Specs); blind B (from
      the proposal, catalog and routed comments, as an adversary hunting edge cases); flip A (red for its stated
      reason); flip B (the simplest wrong build); cohesion, which also lists the whole family up front (every export of
      the touched entries, every sibling of a changed type or helper) and every state with more than one writer. The
      lead checks the mapping and re-runs the cited reds itself; no suite re-run and no fixtures-proof re-check (the
      close review reads the fixtures).
    - A close review's one suite run is the coverage run (`nub run test:coverage`); one sidecar reads the uncovered
      lines and branches in the files the Task changed and names, for each, the Story or edge value that would reach it,
      dead code to delete, or a defect-only path (the user, 2026-11-20). No coverage percentage or baseline. Flip A
      re-runs on the Task's final checks.
    - A close review (R17) runs skill:check-task and reviews the Task's diff in the same pass.
    - Stopping rule (the user, 2026-11-12): a finding blocks only when it is in the Task's range, has a check red on
      current code, and is a lost, doubled, made-up or wrong ending, a hang, or a broken acceptance item; others are
      routed or noted. A fix round is checked by the next review, with no separate re-check. A fix round carries the
      blockers and the user's decisions; other routed items go to their owner Tasks, unless they change the same state
      as a blocker (the user, 2026-11-23).
    - Every accepted finding names why the tests missed it, by R17; its class is covered per @convention.fixtures, and a
      class that recurs across Tasks becomes a run rule or a fixture every later Task inherits (the user, 2026-11-16).
    - Convergence follows skill:policy-convergence (the user, 2026-11-20, after T57; the threshold for a local cause is
      2, the user, 2026-11-12; R35). Tests before convergence (the user, 2026-11-16): first every known behaviour of the
      part gets its fixtures, Stories and tests, the class shown red; only then redesign. On running code the freedom is
      taken away by structure (skill:policy-convergence#converging-live-code, the user, 2026-11-19, after T99).
    - When a whole `nub run faults` misses a fault, it lands as a run rule or a Story test, owned by the Task in range.
    - Every review checks the code it reads for outside imports, temporary names and what it re-creates of Effect; code
      quality is otherwise left to lint (the user, 2026-11-14).
    - Every requirements review and close review runs skill:check-task step 5 (cohesion) (the user, 2026-11-19), with
      this project's house patterns: tokens not strings, `$match` or tag tables, branded ids, `X.use`, Schema-derived
      types. One sidecar takes cohesion as its fixed angle.
    - A ruling is asked as two traces of the same Story, not as prose. A ruling that changes how fixtures or user code
      look comes with complete before/after code (AGENTS.md "Showing designs"). A decision the user doubts gets a fresh
      spike, measured, before it is ruled on again (the user, 2026-11-23).
    - Loop findings (the user, 2026-11-22): at each slice's close the orchestrator also adds to @proposal.loop-findings
      what worked and what should change for future slices, only lessons that apply beyond that slice, each marked
      proposed; the user confirms them, confirmed items are merged into the Epic or a convention and removed from
      the record.
    - At each slice's close the orchestrator writes the user's page. A second slice may not close on an unread page (the
      user, 2026-11-12); that is in stops. On a close review's PASS the orchestrator still sets the Task `done`; only
      the slice's close (its parent, and the page's loop findings) waits on the unread page (the user, 2026-11-23). The
      user's page ends with a "Review the diff" section of `hunk diff <from> <to>` commands for the slice: the whole
      slice, then its requirements commits, its build, and each fix round, then each Task's own changes (with a pathspec
      where Tasks share commits) (the user, 2026-11-22).
    - Benchmarks follow @convention.bench (the user, 2026-11-18): no agent sits blocked on a bench; `--dev` while
      working, every longer run capped and in the background; a Task's close runs no bench by default, and a performance
      Task records the quick tier once at its end, its review reading `--summary`. No full or tier-wide bench run, the
      tooling bench included: only narrow `--only <ids>` cases a Task needs (the user, 2026-11-22; a full run takes
      about 3 hours).

    **Records and commands**

    - An Epic note is 1 to 3 lines; a Task comment is a verdict, the decisions that still matter and their reason (the
      user, 2026-11-11). No progress comments, commit lists or test counts beyond the one revision verified.
    - Command time limit: 5 minutes each (memory, 2026-11-04).
    - No worktrees (the user, 2026-11-08): the builder works in the main checkout on `flow-rewrite`.
    - No agent reads agent transcripts (the user, 2026-11-15).
cadence
    A wake every 60 minutes (the user, 2026-11-17; was 30) checks for a Task in review, a stalled lead, the usage
    reserve and the 5-hour window; no wake starts a bench (the user, 2026-11-22; was the tooling bench every three
    hours). Reviews run on the events in instructions, not on the clock.

    Session cron `7 * * * *`. One job only: the scheduler adds a different fixed delay to each job, so several jobs
    drift apart.

    Prompt: "V3 wake. Read wiki/tasks/V3/epic.task and the last notes in its V3.log. Read the weekly usage and the
    5-hour window from the harness's usage view; if the reserve in Limits is reached, stop the run by R21 and note it;
    follow instructions' 5-hour pacing. Never start a bench or `nub run faults` whole (the user's demand only). If a
    Task is in review with no verdict yet, or a review event in instructions has no review covering it, and no lead is
    running, start one Opus lead at medium effort with its read-only Haiku sidecars, one of them blind; verify its
    findings, route the accepted ones, and note the review. If the lead has shown no activity for 100 minutes, resume it
    by message or replace it (R7) and note it. Otherwise write nothing."

    Quiet rule: a wake that finds nothing writes no note and sends no message; timings within bound are not noted.
stops
    - The user's decisions in the Authority section;
    - a guarantee that can't be met;
    - a slice's close while the previous slice's user's page is unread, which holds only that close;
    - a change proposal whose judge is the user;
    - the user-judged decisions in P5, P6, P7 and P9 (their cards);
    - each ship stage in P10;
    - the real-phone run in P9, which holds only that item.
